insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('46b7940d-69c1-5be6-8537-6411cb6424e3', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["reacts to", "varies from", "helps with", "argues with"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Amaranth grain was domesticated in Mexico. Its physical structure is no longer identical to the structure of the wild plant it is descended from. Summer squash also ______ its wild ancestor. That ancestor plant had a hard rind and bitter flesh. Indigenous people in eastern North America carefully bred the crop until it had a soft rind and mild-tasting flesh.'),
  ('91febf8a-a037-5a59-bd76-392a6a0ba328', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["attractiveness", "utility", "tendency", "frailty"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The adult io moth appears black, orange, and yellow with large eyespots as a result of pigmented scales that cover its wings and body. Although scales are widely known as the source of a moth’s color and pattern, the ______ of these scales goes beyond visual display: they also detach when moths are caught in a spiderweb.'),
  ('1cd0e24f-0793-5c77-b76e-a29d0445b423', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["lauded", "reinterpreted", "evaluated", "criticized"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Despite a growing view among young people and others in South Africa that celebrated anti-apartheid leader Nelson Mandela should have taken greater strides as president to effect more substantive structural changes in the country’s stratified society, years after his death in 2013 Mandela continues to be _______ by many worldwide.'),
  ('9e567d39-e1ff-579f-8d91-73e03c46317c', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["novelty", "shallowness", "rigor", "obscurity"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The dinosaur displays at museums such as the Naturmuseum Senckenberg in Frankfurt (which has a life-sized Diplodocus longus statue among its holdings) are notable for the _______ of the research behind them—the museum staff consulted numerous sources to ensure the accuracy of the displays.'),
  ('fa3c8900-7835-5089-b36e-e8bc7ce5af93', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["It describes a place two characters visited on vacation.", "It illustrates that two characters share a goal.", "It emphasizes a difference between two characters.", "It shows how two characters solved a mystery."]'::jsonb, 5, 'The following text is adapted from Eugene O’Neill’s 1920 play Beyond the Horizon. Andrew and Robert Mayo are brothers who grew up on their family’s farm.

ANDREW: Farming ain’t your nature. There’s all the difference shown in just the way us two feel about the farm. You—well, you like the home part of it, I expect; but as a place to work and grow things, you hate it. Ain’t that right?

ROBERT: Yes, I suppose it is. For you it’s different. You’re a Mayo through and through. You’re wedded to the soil. You’re as much a product of it as an ear of corn is, or a tree. Father is the same. This farm is his life-work.'),
  ('b7bbbda3-6f65-551f-a353-9b80ffcb15f0', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?', '["It summarizes an unexpected finding.", "It describes a debate among historians.", "It gives information about a famous person.", "It explains the meaning of a word."]'::jsonb, 6, 'In the past, historians who wanted to examine Frederick Douglass’s diary and other personal papers had to visit the Library of Congress in Washington, DC, to view them on microfilm (<u>film containing scaled-</u><u>down reproductions of documents</u>). But traveling to the library often added time and costs to research projects. Now, by going to the library’s website, researchers can access digitized versions of Douglass’s papers without physically going anywhere.'),
  ('353b332d-ba6f-51ff-b450-4ec7e7064fe7', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["To discuss the benefits of a new energy technology", "To encourage regional electric utilities to build energy storage facilities", "To point out the differences between two methods of energy generation", "To explain the basics of how a specific energy technology works"]'::jsonb, 7, 'Built in the 1970s, Raccoon Mountain is a pumped-storage hydropower facility (a “water-battery”) located in the United States along the Tennessee River. When energy demand is low, excess power from the regional electric utility’s nuclear plants is used to pump water (from a lower reservoir filled from the Tennessee River) up a shaft to the summit lake, where the water is stored as gravitational potential energy. When energy demand peaks, the water drains down from the summit lake, spinning turbines and generating upward of 1,700 megawatts of power—enough to power one million homes for twenty hours.'),
  ('d7fe7d22-19aa-5673-b8b9-50c8edf018cf', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the overall
characterization in Text 1 of the publicity campaign?', '["By acknowledging that the circumstances of publication for Barrett Browning and Mackay differed from those for the authors whose works are the focus of Text 1, given that Barrett Browning and Mackay each published multiple works", "By asserting that while it is clearly inappropriate to conclude that Barrett Browning was motivated by the same set of factors as the authors of the works discussed in Text 1 to publish under the names they did, it is appropriate to conclude that Mackay was", "By agreeing that the authors of The Roadmender, The Life of Martin R. Delany, and the other works referred to in Text 1 were not afforded the same opportunities as Barrett Browning and Mackay to shape their own representations when publishing", "By suggesting that Barrett Browning and Mackay are among the historical examples that challenge the assumption about the historical use of pseudonyms by female writers that is presented in Text 1"]'::jsonb, 8, '**Text** 1
The Roadmender, The Life of Martin R. Delany, and twenty-three other works of the late 1800s and early 1900s were rereleased in 2020 as publicity for a prestigious prize for women writers of fiction. Recognizing that women long had to use male pseudonyms for their works to be granted serious consideration (by publishers and readers alike), the campaign restored the authors’ given female names in place of such pseudonyms and brought visibility to identities that should not have been hidden.

**Text** 2
The belief that women historically were barred from publishing under names perceived as female is persistent. That it is also an overgeneralization becomes obvious when considering writers such as Elizabeth Barrett Browning, who published Poems and many other works under her own name in the 1800s, and Mary Mackay, who elected to use the female pseudonym Marie Corelli for her novels and stories in the late 1800s.'),
  ('79dac2bd-bb38-5787-b0f0-63eded6cb9d7', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice best states the main topic of the text?', '["Animals without feathers", "Dinosaurs with feathers", "The most valuable fossil", "Bird species"]'::jsonb, 9, 'When people think of dinosaurs with feathers, they typically think of winged dinosaurs, such as the four-winged Microraptor. However, many dinosaurs that didn’t have wings also had feathers on their bodies. For instance, research indicates that the wingless, speedy Ornithomimus likely had feathers.'),
  ('33468a89-521d-53a4-8024-d407321f3ac9', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Based on the text, why does Don Mariano push aside the furniture on the piazza?', '["He is making his home more comfortable for his family.", "He feels that walking an uninterrupted path will help him think through a difficult issue.", "He wishes to create the impression that he often receives important guests at his home.", "He wants to make space so that he can engage in vigorous exercise."]'::jsonb, 10, 'The following text is from María Amparo Ruiz de Burton’s 1885 novel The Squatter and the Don.

Don Mariano Alamar was silently walking up and down the front piazza of his house at the rancho; his hands listlessly clasped behind and his head slightly bent forward in deep thought. He had pushed away to one side the many arm-chairs and wicker rockers with which the piazza was furnished. He wanted a long space to walk. That his meditations were far from agreeable, could easily be seen by the compressed lips, slight frown, and sad gaze of his mild and beautiful blue eyes.'),
  ('2687bf76-9087-5810-9b0f-9b8ec44cc0d8', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?', '["though the percent decrease in hours worked in the United Kingdom was greater than that in Germany and Mexico, it was less than that in Australia.", "while the number of hours worked rose in the United Kingdom from 1950 to 2017, it declined in Germany, Australia, and Mexico.", "the decline in number of hours worked was greater in the United Kingdom than it was in Germany, Australia, or Mexico.", "though the percent decrease in hours worked in the United Kingdom was less than that in Germany, it was greater than that in Australia and Mexico."]'::jsonb, 11, '| Country | 1950 | 2017 | Change in hours | Percent change in hours |
| United Kingdom | 2,184 | 1,670 | −514 | −24% |
| Australia | 2,178 | 1,731 | −447 | −21% |
| Germany | 2,427 | 1,354 | −1,074 | −44% |
| Mexico | 2,432 | 2,255 | −177 | −7% |

Calculations may be inexact due to rounding.

A student in an economics course is examining the decline since 1950 in average hours worked per person per year in various nations due to both increased productivity and the adoption of policies that limit working hours. The first task in this investigation is to determine how the decline in the United Kingdom compares to that in other countries. The student finds that _____'),
  ('589766ce-f500-5911-937f-d9d49fe0b85c', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the comparison?', '["made the lowest total contribution by industry.", "made the highest average economic contribution per employee.", "ranked highest in all three economic measures listed in the table.", "employed the same number of people as construction."]'::jsonb, 12, '| Industry | Approximate total contribution by industry | Number of people employed by industry | Average contribution per employee by industry |
| Construction | \$6,797,300,000 | 77,247 | \$87,994 |
| Manufacturing | \$16,707,500,000 | 128,122 | \$130,403 |
| Tribal economic activity | \$7,312,400,000 | 51,674 | \$141,510 |

The nearly forty tribes located in Oklahoma, including the Osage Nation and the Seminole Nation, operate numerous businesses, employ tens of thousands of people, and generate billions of dollars in revenue. A student in an economics class is researching the tribes’ collective activity as a single industry. The student wants to compare that industry’s role in Oklahoma in 2017 with the roles of other industries in the state. Looking at a table with information about three industries, the student finds that compared with the other two industries, tribal economic activity _______'),
  ('bfc50d29-af58-5335-8cec-e74b3b98cbdb', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which statement, if true, would best illustrate the challenge described in the text?', '["As is also the case with some first-language speakers of Afro-Seminole Creole, many first-language speakers of Hawaiian Pidgin align their pronunciation, word choice, and syntax more closely with English in certain conversational contexts than in others.", "Stative structures in which verbs modify subjects and serve a descriptive function comparable to that of adjectives are hallmarks of Hawaiian Pidgin as well as of East Asian languages such as Korean, yet the syntax of Hawaiian Pidgin’s stative structures confirms their origin in ‘Ōlelo Hawaiʻi.", "Hawaiian Pidgin’s substitution of “d” for the voiced “th” consonant (as used in “that”) and of “t” for the unvoiced “th” consonant (as used in “thick”) can be credited to the absence of “th” consonants in ‘Ōlelo Hawaiʻi, though most other Polynesian languages lack “th” consonants too.", "The usage of the Hawaiian Pidgin verb “ste” to convey a temporary state, as in the statement “da watah ste cold” (the water is cold), most nearly resembles that of the ‘Ōlelo Hawaiʻi verb noho, though the Portuguese verb ficar and the Spanish verb estar have similar usages and may also exert an influence on “ste.”"]'::jsonb, 13, 'A creole language is an amalgam of a lexifier, or primary contributor of grammar and vocabulary, with one or more substrates, or secondary contributors. English-lexifying creoles in North America, such as Afro-Seminole Creole, rely on West African substrates, while Hawaiian Pidgin relies on ‘Ōlelo Hawaiʻi, the Polynesian language of the Native Hawaiian people, with further contributions from Korean and other East Asian languages, Portuguese, and Spanish. This is a heterogeneous history, even for a creole language. Moreover, numerous similarities are found among the East Asian substrates, as well as between Portuguese and Spanish. Thus, it can be challenging to credit certain words or features of grammar or pronunciation to any one substrate.'),
  ('641cc867-e972-54f4-9022-a16ee4947f2a', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["extend the average lifespan of great egrets already in the area than they are to attract a greater number of those birds to the area.", "attract birds that don’t typically live in wetlands to the area than they are to attract great egrets or wood storks to the area.", "attract great egrets to the area than they are to attract wood storks to the area.", "decrease the area’s appeal to both great egrets and wood storks than they are to increase the appeal to either."]'::jsonb, 14, 'The great egret and the wood stork are long-legged birds that live in wetlands, like the Everglades in Florida. Laura D’Acunto and colleagues wanted to know how these birds choose an area in which to live. They looked at features of the birds’ habitats, such as the extent of tree-canopy coverage in the area and how deep the water is during the birds’ breeding season. They found that great egrets prefer areas with deep water during breeding season, but that was not true for wood storks. The researchers concluded that water management strategies that increase the depth of water in potential wetland bird habitats during breeding season are therefore more likely to _______'),
  ('79a29d85-5e4e-57d7-a9f0-0b4e11afa1f5', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the sound wave treatment system may help solve a problem associated with filtration of microplastics.", "combining filtration with sound waves is likely the most efficient method for removing microplastics from water.", "directing water through filtration tubes with multiple outlets reduces microplastic pollution.", "unlike sound waves, filters have the ability to remove large microplastics from polluted waterways."]'::jsonb, 15, 'Filtration is a widely used method for removing microplastics—plastics with a width less than 5 millimeters (mm)—from waterways. However, filtration systems are typically unable to capture very small microplastics. Menake Piyasena and Nelum Perera designed a device that applies sound waves to water as it flows through a metal tube. The sound waves cause microplastics in the water to gather in the middle and along the sides of the tube. The microplastics can then be separated out by channeling polluted and clean water through different outlets. Testing indicated that the system removes even tiny microplastics, including ones as small as 10 microns, or 0.01 mm, wide. This result suggests that ______'),
  ('4b865a4b-f4e5-5de4-bb72-1223a3d61f9c', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["learning trustworthy", "learning. Trustworthy", "learning, trustworthy", "learning, which trustworthy"]'::jsonb, 16, 'A senior data scientist at Apple, Kate Niehaus works on developing artificial intelligence technology, with a specific focus on trustworthy machine ______ machine learning involves teaching computer algorithms to be accurate, safe, and fair.'),
  ('2af1aa09-50b0-5dae-a613-97236f64b756', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["showing", "having shown", "shows", "to show"]'::jsonb, 17, 'The Tuskegee University Archives’ collection includes an original 1957 photograph of Martin Luther King Jr. addressing a group of Tuskegee civil rights activists. Taken by P.H. Polk, the photo ______ King in profile, the glow of a stage light casting his silhouette onto the curtains behind him.'),
  ('abf35bd4-bf54-5d35-88d3-046e931c6416', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["single-handedly,", "single-handedly;", "single-handedly and", "single-handedly"]'::jsonb, 18, 'Sociologist Alton Okinaka sits on the review board tasked with adding new sites to the Hawai‘i Register of Historic Places, which includes Menehune Fishpond and the Wailuku School. However, Okinaka doesn’t make such decisions ______ all historical designations must be approved by a group of nine other experts from the fields of architecture, archaeology, history, and Hawaiian culture.'),
  ('10764e37-56e4-5cb0-ac30-57bb03cb8437', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["ice Ih contains crystals, whereas amorphous ice, which lacks the thermal energy to form them, does not.", "amorphous ice lacks the thermal energy to form the crystals found in ice Ih.", "the lack of thermal energy in amorphous ice explains its inability to form the crystals found in ice Ih.", "ice Ih differs from amorphous ice in that it possesses the thermal energy to form crystals."]'::jsonb, 19, 'Most of the ice found on Earth is ice Ih, distinguished by a crystalline structure in which molecules form a hexagonal pattern. Amorphous ice, on the other hand, constitutes most of the ice in the ultrarigid environment of outer space. Defined by a disorganized molecular structure, ______'),
  ('7911c365-b360-5c80-818a-b38e30924bd0', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["record. Evidence of cloning in", "record: evidence of cloning. In", "record—evidence of cloning in", "record, evidence of cloning, in"]'::jsonb, 20, 'When, in 2018, scientists in Germany discovered a fossilized brittle star with three thick, spine-studded arms positioned opposite three thin, undeveloped ones, they found something never before seen in the fossil ______ clonal fragmentation, a type of asexual reproduction, an animal splits itself in two, each half then regrowing its missing limbs—just what the brittle star was doing.'),
  ('10f7019f-ba50-584d-8f44-58300cb3e654', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["On the other hand,", "Nowadays,", "However,", "By doing so,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'Typically, upon detecting the scent of another animal, a leopard will open its mouth. ________ the leopard allows the scent to reach sensory receptors on the roof of its mouth. These sensors help the leopard interpret the source of the scent.'),
  ('e0dafabb-c2b1-5a1c-bf16-a95be21aa31e', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'The student wants to emphasize a difference between the two seaweeds. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Participants in a research study found sea palm, a type of brown seaweed, to have moderate umami intensity, while ma-konbu’s umami was more intense.", "Both sea palm and ma-konbu are types of brown seaweed.", "Sea palm and ma-konbu both contain umami flavor, which can also be triggered by compounds in anchovies and mushrooms.", "Some types of brown seaweed, like sea palm and ma-konbu, trigger umami flavor in human taste buds."]'::jsonb, 22, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

The human tongue contains taste receptors for a rich, savory flavor called umami.
Umami is triggered by the compounds in a variety of foods, including anchovies and mushrooms. Participants in a study tasted a sample of sea palm, a type of brown seaweed.
They rated its umami intensity as moderate.
The participants tasted a sample of ma-konbu, another type of brown seaweed.
They rated its umami intensity as high.'),
  ('84ddbe1b-9a1a-5967-9919-08118ced15ca', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'The student wants to identify the title of NoViolet Bulawayo’s first published short story. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["NoViolet Bulawayo’s first published short story was called “Hitting Budapest.”", "In 2010, a short story by NoViolet Bulawayo appeared in Boston Review.", "NoViolet Bulawayo’s first published work of fiction appeared in 2010.", "Acclaimed writer NoViolet Bulawayo’s first published work of fiction was a short story."]'::jsonb, 23, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

NoViolet Bulawayo is an acclaimed writer.
Her first published work of fiction was a short story.
It was called “Hitting Budapest.”
It first appeared in Boston Review in 2010.'),
  ('2bedb466-8447-51bd-8a09-515889bad502', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'The student wants to provide an example of a sky island. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Sky islands are isolated mountain ranges whose environments differ drastically from that of the surrounding lowlands; as the USFS puts it, “the mountains are ‘islands’ surrounded by deserts that are ‘seas.’”", "The USFS considers each of the sky islands, whose environments differ drastically from that of the surrounding lowlands, to be a “unique ecosystem.”", "In the southwestern US and northwestern Mexico, there are dozens of sky islands: isolated mountain ranges whose environments differ drastically from that of the surrounding lowlands.", "There are dozens of sky islands in the southwestern US and northwestern Mexico, such as the El Tigre Mountains."]'::jsonb, 24, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

The El Tigre Mountains are a mountain range located in northwestern Mexico.
The range is one of the dozens of “sky islands” in the southwestern US and northwestern Mexico. A sky island is an isolated mountain range whose environment differs drastically from that of the surrounding lowlands.
The US Forest Service (USFS) said, “The mountains are ‘islands’ surrounded by deserts that are ‘seas.’”
The USFS said, “Each Sky Island is a unique ecosystem.”'),
  ('0d1bcfdb-6625-52d0-bb13-d3f0736e7017', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'The student wants to emphasize a difference between the two scrolls. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Ike Taiga created Landscape and Couplet of Chinese Verse, which is made up of two scrolls that each depict nature.", "Made up of two scrolls that each depict nature, Landscape and Couplet of Chinese Verse was created by Li Bai.", "In Landscape and Couplet of Chinese Verse, one scroll features a painting, while the other scroll features a poem.", "One of the two scrolls that make up Landscape and Couplet of Chinese Verse features mountains, and one features a portrait of poet Li Bai."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Ike Taiga (1723–1776) was a Japanese painter.
Li Bai (701–762) was a Chinese poet.
Taiga created the artwork titled Landscape and Couplet of Chinese Verse.
The artwork is made up of two scrolls.
Scroll 1 features a painting of a lone figure walking among tall, narrow mountains.
Scroll 2 features a poem from Li Bai that is about being in nature.'),
  ('e9be3fcc-ae92-54b1-85eb-f2d40c6e2cb2', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'The student wants to provide evidence that McArthur’s journey was a true circumnavigation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["To be considered a true circumnavigation, a journey must begin and end in the same port, and McArthur’s lasted 94 days.", "McArthur is a British sailor who completed a true circumnavigation that ended in Les Sables d’Olonne, France.", "McArthur began and ended her trip in the same port (Les Sables d’Olonne, France), one of the requirements for a true circumnavigation.", "A true circumnavigation, McArthur’s journey also passed the three great capes of the Southern Ocean."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Dame Ellen McArthur is a British sailor who completed a true circumnavigation of the globe in 2001. One of the requirements of a true circumnavigation is that the journey must begin and end in the same port.
McArthur’s circumnavigation began and ended in the port of Les Sables d’Olonne, France. On her journey, McArthur passed the three great capes of the Southern Ocean.
Her journey took 94 days.'),
  ('b1e88564-cc4f-5824-b615-70081e0238aa', 'a458eac1-3b91-5863-aa63-7e86c9bd4864', 'The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Macready and Keene. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Macready managed a London theater, whereas Keene managed one in New York.", "Both Macready and Keene were managers of the Covent Garden Theatre in London, where they also starred in various plays.", "As actor-managers, both Macready and Keene managed theaters where they also performed.", "In the 1800s, some actors (such as Keene) also managed theaters; they were thus considered actor-managers."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

In the 1800s, some actors also managed theaters.
They were known as actor-managers.
William Charles Macready managed the Covent Garden Theatre (London) from 1837 to 1839. He starred in a production of Henry V there in 1838.
Laura Keene managed Laura Keene’s Theatre (New York) from 1856 to 1863.
She starred in a production of The Seven Sisters there in 1860.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('46b7940d-69c1-5be6-8537-6411cb6424e3', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The preceding sentence says amaranth’s physical structure “is no longer identical to the structure of the wild plant it is descended from,” so the second sentence needs a verb phrase that similarly indicates a difference from an ancestor. “Varies from” precisely and concisely means “is different from,” so “Summer squash also varies from its wild ancestor” matches the intended meaning and parallel structure.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “Reacts to” implies a response to an external influence, which doesn’t fit the context describing how a domesticated plant differs from its wild ancestor.
**Choice C is incorrect.** “Helps with” requires an object that benefits from the squash; it does not express a relationship of difference between domesticated and wild forms.
**Choice D is incorrect.** “Argues with” is figurative and anthropomorphic and does not sensibly describe the relationship between a plant and its ancestor.
**Faster approach:** Look for a choice that expresses difference or nonidentity to mirror “no longer identical”; eliminate options that imply action, assistance, or personification to quickly select “varies from.”'),
  ('91febf8a-a037-5a59-bd76-392a6a0ba328', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence contrasts what scales are "widely known" for (visual display) with an additional role they serve. "Utility" precisely names a function or usefulness that could extend beyond appearance, so "the utility of these scales goes beyond visual display" logically introduces the secondary role (detaching in a spiderweb).
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Attractiveness" repeats the idea of appearance rather than providing a contrasting additional role; it would not set up the following clause about detaching in a web.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Tendency" refers to a propensity or likelihood; "the tendency of these scales goes beyond visual display" is ungrammatical and does not convey the intended idea of an additional function.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Frailty" focuses on weakness or fragility. While related to breaking or detaching, it does not fit the intended contrast with visual display as cleanly as "utility," which emphasizes a useful role beyond appearance.
**Faster approach:** Note the contrast signaled by "Although"; choose a noun meaning "function/usefulness" (not another appearance word) to introduce the nonvisual role—this quickly narrows choices to "utility."'),
  ('1cd0e24f-0793-5c77-b76e-a29d0445b423', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence sets up a contrast: although some now think Mandela should have done more, years after his death he still receives positive recognition. The conjunction "Despite" and the phrase "continues to be" signal that the blank should be a favorable, ongoing appraisal. "Lauded" (praised) fits that meaning exactly and matches the global, enduring regard described.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Reinterpreted" means seen in a new way, which is neutral and does not convey the enduring positive esteem implied by "continues to be" in the passage.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Evaluated" is neutral and merely indicates assessment; it fails to capture the sense of sustained praise implied by the context.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Criticized" contradicts the contrast set up by "Despite... continues to be" and would reverse the intended meaning.
**Faster approach:** Use contrast clues—words like "Despite" and "continues" signal that the blank should express an ongoing positive appraisal; pick the answer that means "praised."'),
  ('9e567d39-e1ff-579f-8d91-73e03c46317c', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence says the museum staff "consulted numerous sources to ensure the accuracy of the displays," which signals careful, thorough research; "rigor" precisely captures that quality of being strict, thorough, and exact in research methods.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Novelty" means newness or originality, which is unrelated to the idea of consulting numerous sources to achieve accuracy.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Shallowness" is the opposite of what the sentence describes; consulting numerous sources implies depth, not shallowness.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Obscurity" means being unknown or unclear, which does not follow from using many sources to ensure accuracy.
**Faster approach:** Look for context clues (here, "consulted numerous sources" and "ensure the accuracy") that point to a word meaning thoroughness or strictness; that immediately selects "rigor."'),
  ('fa3c8900-7835-5089-b36e-e8bc7ce5af93', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The exchange explicitly contrasts the brothers’ feelings about the farm: Andrew says Robert “hates” farming while Robert acknowledges that Andrew is “wedded to the soil.” The passage’s purpose is to highlight that difference in attitude between the two characters.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not describe a vacation or travel; it focuses on the brothers’ relationship to their family farm, not a place visited for leisure.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The brothers do not share a goal; instead, their opposing attitudes toward farming are emphasized, so shared purpose is not supported.
**Choice D is incorrect.** No mystery or problem-solving occurs—the dialogue concerns feelings about the farm, not solving anything.
**Faster approach:** Look for contrast words and statements of feeling (for example, "hate" vs. "wedded"), which signal that the passage is emphasizing a difference between characters.'),
  ('b7bbbda3-6f65-551f-a353-9b80ffcb15f0', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The underlined phrase, set off by parentheses and immediately following the term "microfilm," supplies a brief definition—"film containing scaled-down reproductions of documents." Its purpose is to explain the meaning of the technical term so readers understand how researchers previously accessed Douglass’s papers.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The parenthetical phrase does not summarize an unexpected finding; it merely defines "microfilm," not report a surprising result.
**Choice B is incorrect.** There is no discussion of differing viewpoints or debate among historians in the parenthetical; it provides a definition, not a description of controversy.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The phrase does not give information about a person (Frederick Douglass); it clarifies what "microfilm" means.
**Faster approach:** When a short phrase in parentheses follows a technical term, treat it as a definition—pick the choice about explaining meaning.'),
  ('353b332d-ba6f-51ff-b450-4ec7e7064fe7', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage describes how Raccoon Mountain’s pumped-storage system operates: pumping water to a summit lake when demand is low, storing gravitational potential energy, and releasing water to drive turbines when demand peaks. The paragraph focuses on the mechanism and operation of this specific energy technology, which matches a purpose of explaining how it works.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does mention a benefit (powering one million homes for twenty hours), but the primary focus is descriptive explanation of the system’s operation, not an overall discussion of benefits.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The tone is informative rather than persuasive; it does not urge utilities to build such facilities or make recommendations.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not compare pumped-storage hydropower with another generation method; it only describes this one system’s process.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the paragraph primarily explains how something works (explanation), argues for something (persuasion), compares items (comparison), or lists benefits (promotion); this one explains a process, so choose the explanation option.'),
  ('d7fe7d22-19aa-5673-b8b9-50c8edf018cf', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** Text 2 argues that the claim in Text 1 is an overgeneralization and immediately offers specific counterexamples—Elizabeth Barrett Browning (who published under her own name) and Mary Mackay (who chose a female pseudonym)—to show that the historical picture is more complicated. The author of Text 2 would therefore point to Browning and Mackay as examples that challenge the broad assumption presented in Text 1.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Text 2 does not focus on differences in the number of works published; it highlights these authors as counterexamples to the general claim.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Text 2 gives both Browning and Mackay as examples undermining the
overgeneralization, not arguing that one fits but the other does not.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Text 2 disputes the blanket characterization in Text 1 rather than agreeing that some authors were uniformly disadvantaged compared to Browning and Mackay.
**Faster approach:** For central-idea questions, scan for explicit counterexamples or qualifying language in the passage; the correct answer will directly reflect those examples or qualifiers.'),
  ('79dac2bd-bb38-5787-b0f0-63eded6cb9d7', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage discusses dinosaurs that had feathers, giving examples (winged Microraptor and wingless Ornithomimus) and noting that many nonwinged dinosaurs also had feathers. The central idea is clearly about dinosaurs with feathers.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage is about animals that do have feathers, not animals without feathers, so this choice contradicts the passage’s content.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not discuss fossils’ value; it mentions research and examples of feathered dinosaurs but says nothing about monetary or scientific value as the main topic.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Although feathers are associated with birds, the passage specifically addresses dinosaurs with feathers, not modern bird species, so this choice is off topic.
**Faster approach:** Identify the recurring subject words (here “dinosaurs” and “feathers”) and choose the answer that combines those words to state the main topic.'),
  ('33468a89-521d-53a4-8024-d407321f3ac9', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage explicitly says that Don Mariano “had pushed away to one side the many arm-chairs and wicker rockers ... He wanted a long space to walk.” That wording directly links moving the furniture to creating an uninterrupted walking area; the surrounding description of his “deep thought” and “meditations” shows he is troubled and likely trying to think through a difficult issue as he walks.
**Choice A is incorrect.** There is no suggestion he is rearranging the piazza for his family’s comfort; the text emphasizes his personal need for space to walk, not hospitality or comfort for others.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage gives no indication he is preparing to receive guests or to create an impression of importance; instead his actions are private and connected to thinking.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Although he clears space to walk, the description emphasizes meditative walking rather than vigorous exercise; words like “deep thought” and “meditations” contradict the idea of vigorous activity.
**Faster approach:** Locate the sentence explaining the action (“He wanted a long space to walk”) and use the surrounding context (his contemplative mood) to infer purpose—match that directly to the answer choice.'),
  ('2687bf76-9087-5810-9b0f-9b8ec44cc0d8', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The table shows the United Kingdom’s percent change in hours is −24%. That percent decrease is smaller (less negative) than Germany’s −44% and larger (more negative) than Australia’s −21% and Mexico’s −7%, so the statement that the UK’s percent decrease was less than Germany’s but greater than Australia’s and Mexico’s accurately describes the data.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It reverses the comparison with Germany and Australia: the UK’s percent decrease (−24%) is not greater than Germany’s (−44%) and is greater than Australia’s (−21%), so A is wrong.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The UK’s hours declined from 2,184 to 1,670 (−514), so hours did not rise.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The numeric decline in the UK (−514 hours) is smaller than Germany’s decline (−1,074 hours), so the decline was not greater in the UK than in all three listed countries.
**Faster approach:** Compare the percent-change column values directly (or the absolute change column) to see which are larger or smaller; using the percent-change column quickly yields the correct ordering for every such comparison question.'),
  ('589766ce-f500-5911-937f-d9d49fe0b85c', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The table shows the average contribution per employee for tribal economic activity is \$141,510, which is larger than manufacturing’s \$130,403 and construction’s \$87,994, so tribal activity made the highest average economic contribution per employee.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Tribal activity did not make the lowest total contribution: construction’s total (\$6,797,300,000) is lower than tribal activity’s \$7,312,400,000.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Tribal activity did not rank highest in all three measures: it is highest in average contribution per employee but not highest in total contribution or number employed (manufacturing has the highest total; construction and manufacturing employ more people).
**Choice D is incorrect.** The number employed in tribal activity is 51,674, which is not the same as construction’s 77,247.
**Faster approach:** Scan the relevant column (here, “Average contribution per employee”) and pick the largest value to answer comparison questions about averages; for totals or employment, scan those specific columns similarly.'),
  ('bfc50d29-af58-5335-8cec-e74b3b98cbdb', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage says it is challenging to credit particular words or features to any one substrate because multiple substrates (and similarities among them) can produce the same feature. Choice D exemplifies that difficulty by showing a Hawaiian Pidgin verb whose usage most nearly resembles an ‘Ōlelo Hawaiʻi verb but also resembles Portuguese and Spanish verbs, so multiple plausible sources could explain it.
**Choice A is incorrect.** That choice describes sociolinguistic code-switching between English and creole varieties, not the difficulty of attributing features to specific substrates.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It asserts that the syntax of the stative structures confirms an ‘Ōlelo Hawaiʻi origin, which contradicts the passage’s claim that attribution is often uncertain.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although it notes that many Polynesian languages lack “th,” it still claims the substitution “can be credited to” ‘Ōlelo Hawaiʻi, which presents a confident attribution rather than the ambiguous multiple-source problem described in the passage.
**Faster approach:** For evidence/attribution questions, pick the choice that highlights multiple plausible sources or ambiguity among substrates rather than one definitive origin.'),
  ('641cc867-e972-54f4-9022-a16ee4947f2a', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage states that great egrets prefer areas with deep water during the breeding season, while wood storks do not. Therefore, a water-management strategy that increases water depth during breeding season would be more likely to attract great egrets than to attract wood storks.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage discusses habitat preference and attraction, not effects on lifespan; comparing lifespan extension to attraction is unsupported and grammatically awkward.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage concerns wetland species (egrets and storks); there is no basis for claiming deeper water would attract birds that don’t typically live in wetlands.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Nowhere does the passage suggest that increasing water depth would decrease appeal to both species; it specifically says egrets prefer deeper water, so appeal would not decrease for them.
**Faster approach:** Identify which species the text says prefers the manipulated condition (deep water) and choose the answer that says that species will be more affected than the other—match preference to predicted outcome.'),
  ('79a29d85-5e4e-57d7-a9f0-0b4e11afa1f5', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage states that filtration systems are typically unable to capture very small microplastics and then describes a sound-wave device that can remove particles as small as 10 microns. The tested ability of the sound-wave system to remove tiny microplastics directly suggests it could address the specific problem—filters’ inability to capture very small particles—that the passage identifies.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not claim that combining filtration with sound waves is the most efficient method, only that the sound-wave device can remove very small particles; efficiency relative to other methods is not established.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not discuss filtration tubes with multiple outlets reducing pollution; it describes a particular sound-wave tube but gives no general claim about multiple outlets.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage does not contrast filters’ ability to remove large microplastics with sound waves; it only notes filters’ weakness with very small particles and the sound device’s strength with tiny particles.
**Faster approach:** Match the passage’s stated problem to the answer that directly addresses that problem—eliminate choices that introduce unsupported broader claims.'),
  ('4b865a4b-f4e5-5de4-bb72-1223a3d61f9c', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage needs a sentence break between the clause describing Niehaus’s focus and the definition that follows. Inserting a period and starting a new sentence—"trustworthy machine learning. Trustworthy machine learning involves..."—produces two grammatically correct sentences. Although the phrase is repetitive, it conforms to Standard English conventions and clearly separates the descriptive clause from the explanatory clause.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "learning trustworthy" would leave two noun phrases back-to-back with no punctuation, creating an awkward and ungrammatical run-on.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "learning, trustworthy" creates a comma splice and fails to connect the explanatory clause properly to the prior clause.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "learning, which trustworthy" is ungrammatical because "which" would require a clause (e.g., "which involves...") and the word order is incorrect.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second part is a separate explanatory sentence; if so, insert a period and capitalize the next word (end the first sentence). This fixes run-ons and comma splices for similar sentence-division problems.'),
  ('2af1aa09-50b0-5dae-a613-97236f64b756', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence needs a finite verb that agrees with the subject "the photo." "Shows" is the present-tense verb that completes the clause: "Taken by P.H. Polk, the photo shows King in profile, the glow of a stage light casting his silhouette onto the curtains behind him." The structure correctly links the participial phrase "Taken by P.H. Polk" to the noun it modifies (the photo) and then uses an independent verb to describe what the photo does.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Showing" would create a dangling modifier or an incomplete clause because it would leave the sentence without a main verb for the subject "the photo," resulting in an ungrammatical fragment.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Having shown" is a perfect participle that does not provide the necessary main verb and would make the sentence awkward and ungrammatical in context.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "To show" is an infinitive and would not function as the main verb for the subject "the photo," leaving the sentence without a proper finite verb.
**Faster approach:** Identify the subject ("the photo") and choose the option that provides a matching finite verb; eliminate non-finite forms (gerund, participle, infinitive) when a main verb is required.'),
  ('abf35bd4-bf54-5d35-88d3-046e931c6416', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The clause that follows—"all historical designations must be approved by a group of nine other experts…"—is an independent clause. To join two closely related independent clauses without a coordinating conjunction, a semicolon is required; so "single-handedly;" correctly separates the clauses while signaling their close relationship.
**Choice A is incorrect.** A comma before the second independent clause would create a comma splice (a run-on sentence) because the clauses are not joined by a coordinating conjunction.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Adding "and" produces an awkward and ungrammatical coordination: the two clauses do not share a single verb structure that would make "and" appropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** No punctuation between the clauses produces a fused sentence that is grammatically incorrect.
**Faster approach:** When two independent clauses appear side by side, use a semicolon to join them if there is no coordinating conjunction; this immediately signals choice B in similar questions.'),
  ('10764e37-56e4-5cb0-ac30-57bb03cb8437', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The introductory participial phrase "Defined by a disorganized molecular structure," must modify the noun that immediately follows; choice B begins with "amorphous ice," so the sentence reads clearly and grammatically: "Defined by a disorganized molecular structure, amorphous ice lacks the thermal energy to form the crystals found in ice Ih." This preserves logical meaning and Standard English conventions.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It begins with "ice Ih," so the participial phrase would wrongly modify ice Ih, creating a dangling modifier.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It begins with "the lack of thermal energy," so the modifier would attach to that noun phrase rather than to "amorphous ice," making the sentence ungrammatical.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It starts with "ice Ih," again causing the introductory phrase to modify the wrong subject and producing a dangling modifier.
**Faster approach:** Check which choice places the noun that the participial phrase should modify immediately after the comma; pick the option that begins with that noun (here, "amorphous ice").'),
  ('7911c365-b360-5c80-818a-b38e30924bd0', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence needs a clear break after "fossil record" and an independent clause explaining clonal fragmentation. Using a colon followed by a sentence gives that break: "...in the fossil record: evidence of cloning. In clonal fragmentation, a type of asexual reproduction, an animal splits itself..." The colon introduces the explanation, and the following sentence correctly begins with "In clonal fragmentation," which governs the parenthetical phrase "a type of asexual reproduction."
**Choice A is incorrect.** Putting a period followed by "Evidence of cloning in" produces an ungrammatical continuation because "in clonal fragmentation" must start the explanatory clause; the phrasing becomes awkward and incomplete.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The em dash option yields "evidence of cloning in clonal fragmentation," which is repetitive and makes the long appositive awkward; it also fails to provide the clean sentence break that clarifies the explanatory clause.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Commas create a run-on string of phrases and do not provide the necessary separation before the explanatory clause; the result is confusing and less grammatical than choice B.
**Faster approach:** When a sentence needs an explanation followed by an independent clause beginning with a prepositional phrase (like "In clonal fragmentation"), use a colon to introduce the explanation and then start a new sentence with the prepositional phrase.'),
  ('10f7019f-ba50-584d-8f44-58300cb3e654', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** "By doing so," introduces a clear result or purpose of the leopard opening its mouth: it allows scent to reach sensory receptors. The second sentence explains the effect of the behavior, so a transition indicating result fits best.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "On the other hand," signals a contrast between two different ideas, but the second sentence continues to explain the same behavior rather than presenting an opposing point.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Nowadays," refers to time or a modern trend; the passage is describing an animal behavior and the following sentence explains purpose, so a temporal transition is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "However," introduces contrast, but the second sentence does not contradict the first —it elaborates on its purpose—so "However," is not logical.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second sentence shows result/purpose (use "By doing so,") or contrast (use "however,"/"on the other hand"); match the transition to the relationship between sentences.'),
  ('e0dafabb-c2b1-5a1c-bf16-a95be21aa31e', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** It directly contrasts the two seaweeds by reporting that participants rated sea palm’s umami intensity as moderate and ma-konbu’s as more intense, which uses the notes’ specific ratings to emphasize a difference.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It merely states that both are types of brown seaweed and does not emphasize any difference in umami intensity.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although accurate about umami and other foods that trigger it, this choice emphasizes similarity and additional examples rather than the difference in intensity between the two seaweeds.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This general statement describes that some brown seaweeds trigger umami but does not highlight the contrasting umami intensities of sea palm and ma-konbu.
**Faster approach:** Scan for wording that explicitly contrasts the two items (words like "while," "more," "less," or comparative ratings) and eliminate choices that only state similarities or general facts.'),
  ('84ddbe1b-9a1a-5967-9919-08118ced15ca', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The student’s stated goal is to identify the title of NoViolet Bulawayo’s first published short story; Choice A explicitly gives that title: “Hitting Budapest.” This directly and fully answers the question using the notes.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although it gives relevant context (year and publication), it does not name the short story, so it fails to identify the title.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This states when the work appeared but does not specify that it was a short story title or give the title itself, so it doesn’t meet the goal of identifying the title.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This confirms the form of the work (a short story) but provides no title, so it does not identify the story’s name.
**Faster approach:** For questions asking for a specific fact (like a title), pick the answer that explicitly states that fact rather than one that gives related context (date, publication, or form).'),
  ('2bedb466-8447-51bd-8a09-515889bad502', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** It both names a specific example from the notes (the El Tigre Mountains) and places that example in the correct context (dozens of sky islands in the southwestern US and northwestern Mexico), directly showing an instance of a sky island as the student intends.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it gives a clear definition and a supporting USFS quote, it does not provide a concrete example from the student’s notes.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It paraphrases the USFS statement about uniqueness and restates the definition, but it does not offer a specific example such as the El Tigre Mountains.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It defines sky islands and gives their location but, like A and B, fails to provide a named example; the prompt asks specifically for an example.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis questions asking for an example, immediately eliminate choices that only define or describe and choose the one that includes a named instance from the provided notes.'),
  ('0d1bcfdb-6625-52d0-bb13-d3f0736e7017', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The notes state that the artwork is made up of two scrolls and that Scroll 1 features a painting while Scroll 2 features a poem by Li Bai, so the most effective way to emphasize a difference is to state that one scroll is a painting and the other is a poem.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although both scrolls involve nature, this choice emphasizes similarity rather than the requested difference between the two scrolls.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This choice misattributes the creation of the artwork to Li Bai; the notes identify Ike Taiga as the painter who created the work.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The notes say one scroll contains a poem by Li Bai, not a portrait of him, so describing the second scroll as a portrait is inaccurate.
**Faster approach:** Locate the two lines that describe each scroll and form a single sentence that contrasts their most distinct features (here, painting vs. poem); choosing the answer that explicitly states that contrast will reliably be correct.'),
  ('e9be3fcc-ae92-54b1-85eb-f2d40c6e2cb2', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** It directly connects the requirement for a true circumnavigation (that the journey begin and end in the same port) with the specific evidence from the notes that McArthur began and ended her trip in Les Sables d’Olonne, France, thereby showing she met that requirement.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it states the requirement, it pairs that requirement with how long McArthur’s voyage lasted (94 days), which is irrelevant to proving a true circumnavigation.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It calls McArthur’s voyage a true circumnavigation but offers only that it ended in Les Sables d’Olonne; it fails to state that the trip both began and ended there or to cite the explicit requirement.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The phrasing is ungrammatical and it fails to link the relevant requirement (beginning and ending in the same port) to McArthur’s actual itinerary; passing the three great capes alone does not establish the required condition.
**Faster approach:** Look for the choice that explicitly pairs the stated requirement with the corresponding specific evidence from the notes; eliminate options that add irrelevant details or fail to make that connection.'),
  ('b1e88564-cc4f-5824-b615-70081e0238aa', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The notes identify both William Charles Macready and Laura Keene as managers of specific theaters and also note that each starred in productions at the theaters they managed (Macready in Henry V in 1838; Keene in The Seven Sisters in 1860). Stating that they were "actor-managers" who managed theaters where they performed accurately synthesizes the shared trait.
**Choice A is incorrect.** That sentence highlights a contrast in location rather than emphasizing a similarity between the two individuals.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The choice falsely claims both managed the Covent Garden Theatre; only Macready did, while Keene managed a New York theater.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The sentence focuses on the general phenomenon and names only Keene, failing to emphasize the parallel between Macready and Keene.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis questions, look for a concise "both" statement that uses facts listed for each subject (e.g., both X and Y did Z); eliminate options that introduce contrasts, incorrect specifics, or mention only one subject.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

-- PeaklyTest Sep 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 2
insert into public.tests (id, title, section, description, duration_min, position, published, form_title, module_no)
values ('5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'PeaklyTest Sep 2025 · Reading & Writing · Module 2', 'Reading & Writing', 'Reading & Writing, Module 2 of the Sep 2025 PeaklyTest · 27 questions · 32 minutes', 32, 191, true, 'PeaklyTest Sep 2025 · Reading & Writing', 2)
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, section = excluded.section, description = excluded.description,
  duration_min = excluded.duration_min, position = excluded.position, form_title = excluded.form_title, module_no = excluded.module_no;
insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('929cf77d-2540-57d6-837f-a22e599d87a9', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["sanitized", "precluded", "estimated", "populated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Noah Fierer and colleagues ________ pots of sterilized soil with slurries of live microbes collected from soil in five sites across Colorado, including areas of sagebrush and ponderosa pine forest. Fierer and team then grew mustard plants in the pots to see if the different microbial slurries affected levels of spicy glucosinolates like 3-methylthiopropyl in the plants’ seeds.'),
  ('e6d87594-22dd-5b5e-91e5-51ac6fe9a8f0', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["exchange", "forecast", "avoid", "monitor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The collectibles market is one of the most difficult segments of the consumer economy to _________. Few economists would have predicted, for example, that the prices of vintage G.I. Joe action figures would soar in the 2010s, but soar they did.'),
  ('be687b35-06f4-52b7-9754-8cfdd3b17d88', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["rectified", "substantiated", "inculcated", "hypothesized"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Derived from research conducted with factory workers from 1924 to 1933, the Hawthorne effect suggests that participants’ awareness that they are being studied alters their behavior and influences study outcomes. Since then, several researchers have claimed to invalidate this phenomenon, positing that the Hawthorne effect cannot be ________ because attempts to detect it invariably involve faulty research methods.'),
  ('0ca12792-8232-5227-9947-0ec0ed00855d', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["harbingers of", "proxies for", "emissaries for", "subordinates of"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Paleontologists think that Ornithomimus, Phuwiangosaurus, and other long-extinct theropods and sauropods may have breathed using air sacs connected to tubelike extensions inside the animals’ bones. Such structures are found in modern birds, which is why some paleontologists tread the respiratory systems of birds as ______ those of Ornithomimus, Phuwiangosaurus, and other theropods and sauropods.'),
  ('f57f8493-2010-5ad3-8eca-9eb3c3f1c7b7', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It details the near-consensus among researchers in a particular field of study regarding how technology evolves and then indicates the controversial nature of a study challenging that broadly accepted view.", "It advances a claim made by researchers in one academic field about the nature of technological change and then critiques a contrasting claim presented by a researcher from a related academic field.", "It explains that a particular interpretation of technological development has been perpetuated in an academic field and then provides a counterexample demonstrating that the interpretation isn’t always accurate.", "It summarizes the findings of several studies into the origins of a particular invention and then presents additional evidence from a more recent study that broadens those findings."]'::jsonb, 5, 'Advancements like the emergence of glassmaking in Mesopotamia circa 3500 BCE are overemphasized in innovation studies, contributing to the idea that technological change always brings greater complexity. Research by Nathaniel Erb-Satullo reveals an important exception: gold metallurgy flourished in the Caucasus in the Bronze Age, but a steep drop during that time (circa 1500 BCE) in objects featuring gold filigree (in which fine threads of gold are arranged in intricate patterns) and other sophisticated goldsmithing techniques suggests that simpler processes supplanted advanced methods.'),
  ('5c506f66-a445-54c8-b55e-6e8aa206fe51', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["To present evidence from Melis et al.’s study in support of the efficacy of their CNN model", "To compare results obtained by Melis et al. using their CNN model to prior results obtained from other researchers’ models", "To account for Melis et al.’s reliance in their study on a CNN model in lieu of direct measurement", "To provide an overview of how Melis et al. honed the accuracy of their CNN model"]'::jsonb, 6, 'In their study of the steering muscles regulating sclerites (minute hardened structures) in the Drosophila (fruit fly) wing hinge, Johan M. Melis et al. used advanced machine learning to devise a convolutional neural network (CNN) model capable of predicting the pattern of wing motion produced by the maximum activity of the muscles. The CNN model’s output aligned with results of prior studies by other researchers measuring muscle activity patterns directly—one of several indications, said Melis et al., that the model accurately represents important biomechanical processes underlying wing motion.'),
  ('efec263c-53b8-59a1-868a-7dcd9628ef05', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Based on the text, why does Miguel accompany his brother to the sisters’ house one day?', '["Miguel’s brother has shared with him all the knowledge he has, and Miguel wants to learn from the sisters now.", "Miguel wishes to share his poems with the sisters and ask them for feedback on his writing.", "Miguel anticipates having the opportunity to be introduced to both the sisters and their father.", "Miguel has not received formal instruction in poetry and wants to ask the sisters’ famous father to be his mentor."]'::jsonb, 7, 'The following text is from Julia Alvarez’s 2000 novel, In the Name of Salomé. The narrator and her sister, daughters of a famous poet, are being tutored by Alejandro Román.

Our tutor, Alejandro Román, would bring his younger brother, Miguel, to class one day. By now I was eighteen and had turned down enough suitors to acquire a reputation of being aloof. Miguel also was studying poetry, and he had heard from his brother that the Ureña girls were none other than the daughters of Nicolás Ureña, and they were smart as clockwork. Miguel was hoping not only to meet us but to make the acquaintance of the poet himself at Mamá’s house.'),
  ('0b7fcec3-41f4-5e58-a81e-3d3236206fed', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'What does the text most strongly suggest about paintings by Roberts?', '["Roberts’s reliance on the technique of fast painting likely accounts for his works being more aesthetically interesting than works by Walker are.", "Although it is evident that Roberts adopted some of Hair’s preferred techniques, Roberts’s works are less derivative of works by Hair than is typically acknowledged.", "The lack of precision with which they were executed suggests that they are inferior to works by either Hair or Walker.", "Because of the manner in which they were created, they likely have visual qualities that are regarded as more typical of Florida Highwaymen paintings than the qualities in works by Walker are."]'::jsonb, 8, 'Motivated to sell as many paintings as possible, Alfred Hair, an influential figure among the landscape artists known as the Florida Highwaymen, pioneered “fast painting,” which in part involved swift applications of paint. That many of Hair’s acolytes, including Livingston Roberts, imitated the technique accounts in part for the impressionistic qualities that are now synonymous with the group’s shared aesthetic. But not all Highwaymen fully embraced this approach; for instance, though Charles Walker was also prolific, his paintings were executed with greater attention to detail.'),
  ('6ccbec76-fdbc-537b-b387-f2518de8ec02', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the assertion?', '["those that also filed Form 4720 paid a larger penalty for failing to meet a minimum charitable distribution requirement than those organizations that filed Form 990 but also filed Form 4720 for the same reason.", "a smaller percentage of those that also filed Form 4720 did so because they held excess interests in a business enterprise than the percentage of those that filed Form 4720 because they did not meet the minimum charitable distribution requirement.", "those that were also required to file Form 4720 because they had excess holdings in a business enterprise paid, on average, a larger penalty than those organizations that filed Form 4720 because they made taxable expenditures.", "those that also filed Form 4720 collectively paid larger penalties for failing to meet the minimum charitable distribution requirement than for other reasons."]'::jsonb, 9, '![figure](/q/sep25/001.webp)

While US public charities, like Kaiser Foundation Hospitals, must file Form 990 yearly with the IRS, private foundations, such as the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, must file a different form, 990-PF. In addition, foundations that engage in certain prohibited activities must also file Form 4720 and pay a penalty tax on the money involved. Private foundations are prohibited from holding excess interests in a business enterprise, “self-dealing” (conducting activities that benefit foundation insiders), making taxable expenditures such as outlays for lobbying, and failing to cross a required threshold in making charitable distributions from income. Out of the organizations that filed Form 990-PF in the years 2003–2005, _____'),
  ('21321c74-cc25-5635-b0e3-020b0b3c04d7', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Assuming that the Volga and Don basins supported similarly sized hunter-gatherer populations 6,500–8,000 years ago, which finding, if true, would most directly support Courel and colleagues’ explanation?', '["The people of the Volga basin acquired the techniques used to create pottery for cooking and storing food from the people of the Don basin.", "Across the Volga and Don basins, people had broadly similar access to the same terrestrial and aquatic animal resources.", "There were many more bodies of water in a comparably sized area in the Don basin than in the Volga basin.", "In both the Volga and Don basins, most of the sites from which pottery has been recovered appear to have been seasonal fishing and hunting encampments rather than year-round settlements."]'::jsonb, 10, 'Blandine Courel and her colleagues analyzed pottery fragments from thirty-five sites across the Volga and Don river basins to determine whether the ways in which hunter-gatherer societies used pottery in these regions around 6,500–8,000 years ago were influenced primarily by local food availability or primarily by cultural factors. Analysis of organic residues on the pottery fragments showed different prevailing uses for pottery in these locations—cooking and storing terrestrial animal protein at Volga sites and cooking and storing aquatic animal protein at Don sites—which Courel and colleagues attribute to cultural differences.'),
  ('116559ba-b64e-55f3-932a-3cf6e48b9931', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scientist’s conclusion?', '["For both toluene and trichloromethane, the BZMA-derived resin exhibited only modestly lower absorption capacity than the resin synthesized from β-cyclodextrin but higher absorption capacity than other resins consisting of commercially available methacrylate monomers.", "Relative to the resin synthesized from β-cyclodextrin and to other resins consisting of commercially available methacrylate monomers, the BZMA-derived resin exhibited superior absorption capacity for toluene but not for trichloromethane.", "Whereas the resin synthesized from β-cyclodextrin exhibited the highest absorption capacity for toluene, the BZMA-derived resin and other resins consisting of commercially available methacrylate monomers exhibited the highest absorption capacity for trichloromethane.", "For both toluene and trichloromethane, the BZMA-derived resin exhibited similar absorption capacity as other resins consisting of commercially available methacrylate monomers and a slightly higher absorption capacity than the resin synthesized from β-cyclodextrin."]'::jsonb, 11, 'To boost the performance of oil-absorbing resins, which are used to remove oil and other organic compounds from wastewater, one team of materials researchers created a resin with a novel—albeit very expensive—monomer (a molecule that can chemically bond with analogous molecules to form polymers) synthesized from β-cyclodextrin. Other researchers have produced resins consisting of various much cheaper, commercially available methacrylate monomers, including one derived from benzyl methacrylate (BZMA). Testing all these resins’ capacity to absorb toluene and trichloromethane, two organic compounds, a scientist concluded that when practical considerations were taken into account, the BZMA-derived resin showed the greatest potential for use in wastewater cleanup.'),
  ('baa51b2f-52db-557a-9f1b-8d5fb5e4cd34', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the characteristics that made the seeds desirable for inclusion in the proposed study may be unknown to some members of the seeds’ social networks.", "most seeds’ social networks include some people who do not know one another and who share few characteristics.", "the seeds’ social networks tend to be somewhat smaller than the networks of people who do not live in rural areas.", "the seeds’ social networks include a high proportion of people who share characteristics with the seeds."]'::jsonb, 12, 'Northern New York’s Hamilton County is among the most rural counties in the United States: the US Census Bureau classified it as 100% rural in 2010. Researchers often struggle to recruit residents of counties like Hamilton for inclusion in studies. Melissa Velieiros and colleagues tested whether snowball sampling improves recruitment. Working in two rural counties, they recruited a few people (known as “seeds”) with the characteristics desired for a proposed study and asked them to recruit additional participants from their social networks. Though the seeds were given minimal guidance, many more people they recruited had the desired characteristics for the study than would be expected by chance alone, most likely because ____________'),
  ('17a5306f-04f4-57e7-a798-b5de65c12d5a', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["bringing female perspectives to the forefront is not indicative of a novel attitude regarding fidelity to Greek and Roman myths’ ideologies.", "the complex motivations given to female characters in modern retellings of Greek and Roman myths reflect a recent shift toward psychological depth in fictional representation.", "purists are overlooking a long tradition of adapting Greek and Roman myths to focus on female characters.", "modern writers’ foregrounding of female characters is chiefly motivated by a desire to counterbalance the primacy of male perspectives among earlier adaptations of Greek and Roman myths."]'::jsonb, 13, 'In classical Greek and Roman mythology, female characters are typically cast as either villains lacking in psychological depth or passive victims who are marginal to these stories, which usually focus on the exploits of male characters. Recently, a subgenre has emerged in which writers reimagine these stories from the perspectives of their female characters, giving them agency and complex motivations. Purists argue that such efforts represent a distinctively modern tendency to impose our own values on past civilizations, obscuring those civilizations’ beliefs. Defenders of the subgenre counter that reimaginings of the myths for new cultural contexts are almost as old as the myths themselves, suggesting that _______'),
  ('b4cc8132-908f-589c-b890-3055ce05829d', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["technologies, in Iceland,", "technologies in Iceland:", "technologies: in Iceland,", "technologies in Iceland,"]'::jsonb, 14, 'The world’s many geothermal power plants leverage an array of ______ the Hellisheidi plant uses a binary cycle system that transforms high-temperature geothermal fluid into electricity, the Darajat plant’s dry steam system pumps superheated steam from a reservoir, and in Turkey, the Pamukoren plant’s flash steam system uses lower-temperature geothermal fluid in conjunction with a secondary fluid.'),
  ('5cc7a852-17b9-536e-86f9-e8ae2f71408b', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["they often become", "this often becomes", "it often becomes", "these often become"]'::jsonb, 15, 'When a given term—“self-fulfilling prophecies” and “role models” are two well-known examples—is generally accepted and frequently used, _______ susceptible to obliteration by incorporation (OBI). In cases of OBI, widely used terms are rarely, if at all, attributed to the individuals who coined them.'),
  ('5cfe54bf-7719-5862-a3b3-06b81cc620ab', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["currency in favor of a direct trade system", "currency—in favor of a direct trade system", "currency, in favor of a direct trade system", "currency in favor of a direct trade system."]'::jsonb, 16, 'In ancient Rome, salt was used as a commodity currency. By using specific goods like salt as common units of exchange, commodity currency economies streamline trade, which is why they often replaced barter economies. Barter economies eschew ________ that requires what economist W.S. Jevons deems a “double coincidence of wants”—in other words, each trading party must want precisely what the other has.'),
  ('74552689-020d-5fb2-a95a-0687797559e5', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["focused", "focus", "focuses", "focusing"]'::jsonb, 17, 'In their attempt to create a quantum random number generator, K. Muhammed Shafi et al. used a continuous-wave diode laser to fire photons at a periodically-poled potassium titanyl phosphate (PPKTP) nonlinear crystal. A plano-convex lens _______ the laser on the center of the 10-millimeter-long crystal ensured a spot size (a measure of the beam’s diameter) of 85 micrometers.'),
  ('bed8d879-e34b-5616-9d9d-6b8e572ebe10', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["punctuates", "punctuate", "punctuating", "are punctuated by"]'::jsonb, 18, 'For her installation The Last Cruze, photographer LaToya Ruby Frazier traveled to Lordstown, Ohio, where she documented the lives of workers at the local automobile plant. In the installation, text culled from a series of in-depth interviews with employees and their families __________ sixty-seven gelatin silver prints, highlighting the collaborative, documentary nature of Frazier’s work.'),
  ('e0a4c753-cd86-58d0-9336-bab931a13723', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["species; the", "species, the", "species. The", "species, and the"]'::jsonb, 19, 'A single specimen of G. hexagonus, collected at a depth of 483 fathoms (884 meters) in the Pacific Ocean, and a single specimen of G. rubescens, collected at a depth of 769 fathoms (1407 meters) in the Indian Ocean, have been preserved as exemplars of their respective _____________ former in a repository at Washington, DC’s Museum of Natural History and the latter in a repository at the Netherlands’ Naturalis Biodiversity Center.'),
  ('00c04f0c-b73d-5fed-8881-a64a1653394b', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["area; the shesep, a unit of length;", "area: the shesep, a unit of length;", "area, the shesep, a unit of length;", "area, the shesep; a unit of length,"]'::jsonb, 20, 'Writing in hieroglyphs, ancient Egyptians recorded measurements using units such as the sa, a unit of ___________ and the khar, a unit of volume. The sa, for instance, is written as a hieroglyph that looks like a duck or goose.'),
  ('c8a16290-ca67-55d7-8075-ec57ddeeaefd', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["At that time,", "In other words,", "However,", "For example,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'While most animals prefer the safety of nighttime migration, many poisonous amphibians—a category that includes the striped newt and the Asian common toad—safely engage in migratory behavior during the day. __________ with the sun in the sky, the amphibians’ striking color patterns deter visually oriented daytime predators by serving as a warning of toxicity.'),
  ('e1874d5b-6951-57dd-87f6-3994f5f1f5ea', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["As a result,", "On the contrary,", "Of course,", "In particular,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'In a given rock formation, Chattian rock from 27.8 million years ago might directly abut Roadian rock from 273 million years ago, with millions of years of material missing in between. ___________ time did not stand still during these intervening years; the unaccounted-for sedimentary material was likely removed from the stratigraphic record via erosion and weathering.'),
  ('be59daeb-e7d6-5052-a152-b3ef9ff01781', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Consequently,", "For example,", "Regardless,", "In fact,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'Architect Victor Gruen, the designer of Indiana’s Woodmar Plaza shopping mall, conceived of the mall as an indoor version of the European town square, a communal space that encouraged visitors to stroll and linger. _________ he designed Woodmar Plaza with long, pedestrian-friendly promenades and ample seating areas clustered around fountains and greenery.'),
  ('8737501a-ace5-5e19-b1c5-1a0993588a4c', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Confirming this hypothesis,", "Drawing a similar conclusion,", "Undermining this explanation,", "Contrary to this phenomenon,"]'::jsonb, 24, 'Long thought to be sessile (immobile), adult Chelonibia testudinaria, barnacles that adhere to sea turtle shells, have been observed to shift slightly in position over time—a phenomenon that has been attributed to the barnacles’ passive displacement by water currents. __________ a research team found that adult C. testudinaria moved toward the heads of their sea turtle hosts and thus against the prevailing water flow, behavior consistent with self-initiated locomotion.'),
  ('0cb3f1f4-2929-552a-b0f5-0e5e0d35b52e', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["To that end,", "Hence,", "Ultimately,", "Moreover,"]'::jsonb, 25, 'In Annie Dillard’s Pilgrim at Tinker Creek—where, early on, the author marvels at a single goldfish’s delicate fins but later winces when imagining a horde of goldfish laying and eating their own eggs—Dillard struggles to reconcile the complicated juxtapositions of the natural world. ____________ nature’s mesmerizing intricacy and pitiless harshness prove inextricably linked for Dillard, like “two branches of the same creek.”'),
  ('b3af866c-0eb6-5b4c-946e-6c7079becfc7', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize a difference between the two movies?', '["Richard Thorpe’s 1929 film The Fatal Warning is among the many lost films of the era.", "Richard Thorpe’s The Fatal Warning (1929) and Oscar Micheaux’s Within Our Gates (1920) are just two examples of silent films from the 1920s.", "While Richard Thorpe’s The Fatal Warning is considered lost to history, Oscar Micheaux’s film Within Our Gates is archived at the UCLA Film Television Archive in Los Angeles, California.", "Oscar Micheaux’s film Within Our Gates can be found at the UCLA Film Television Archive in Los Angeles, California."]'::jsonb, 26, 'Silent films can be valuable historical documents of their time
Ninety percent of silent films made before 1930 are now lost.
A film is considered lost when no remaining copies are known to exist
Director Richard Thorpe’s 1929 silent film The Fatal Warning is lost.
Director Oscar Micheaux’s 1920 silent film Within Our Gates is archived at the UCLA Film Television Archive in Los Angeles, California.'),
  ('385029ea-3ee9-5b94-b20a-722e2f443bee', '5efe9371-cc55-5c4f-82e0-6279a0a259fc', 'The student wants to contrast the type of neurotechnology EMOTIV develops with the type of neurotechnology Saluda Medical develops. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Unlike Saluda Medical, which develops neurotechnology, EMOTIV develops BCIs.", "BCIs interpret and execute brain signals to allow users to control external software or hardware and execute brain signals to allow users to control external software or hardware with their thoughts, whereas neuromodulation technologies restore sensory, motor, or neural functions.", "Founded in 2011, EMOTIV develops technology for interpreting and executing brain signals, which, unlike Saluda Medical and BrainGate, is categorized as a neuroprosthodontic technology.", "BCIs allow users to control external software or hardware with their thoughts, but they don’t influence neural activity as neuromodulation technologies do."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Founded in 2011, EMOTIV is a neurotechnology company that develops brain-computer interfaces (BCIs).
BCIs interpret and execute brain signals to allow users to control external software or hardware with their thoughts.
Founded in 2013, Saluda Medical is a neurotechnology company that develops neuromodulation technologies.
Neuromodulation technologies use neural interfaces to stimulate nervous system structures and influence neural activity.
Founded in 2009, BrainGate is a neurotechnology company that develops neuroprostheses. Neuroprostheses act as replacement brain functions to restore the user’s lost sensory, motor, or neural functions.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('929cf77d-2540-57d6-837f-a22e599d87a9', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence describes applying slurries of live microbes to previously sterilized soil; to "populate" pots of sterilized soil with microbes precisely captures the action of introducing and establishing microbial communities in those pots. The verb fits grammatically and logically with the experimental procedure that follows.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Sanitized" is redundant because the soil is already described as sterilized; moreover, it would mean making the soil sterile rather than adding microbes to it.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Precluded" means prevented, which contradicts the described experiment of introducing microbes into the soil.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Estimated" means judged or calculated and does not describe physically adding microbial slurries to the pots.
**Faster approach:** Focus on the verb’s meaning in context—identify whether the experiment requires introducing microbes (add/populate) versus measuring or preventing; eliminate verbs that conflict with "sterilized soil."'),
  ('e6d87594-22dd-5b5e-91e5-51ac6fe9a8f0', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence sets up that the collectibles market is hard to predict; the example that follows—unexpected soaring prices for vintage G.I. Joe figures—illustrates unpredictability. "Forecast" precisely means to predict future trends or prices, so it completes the sentence logically and concisely.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Exchange" means to trade or swap; that meaning does not fit the idea signaled by the example about surprising price movements.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Avoid" means to stay away from, which is not supported by the example about unpredictable price behavior.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Monitor" means to watch or track; while related to markets, the passage emphasizes difficulty in predicting outcomes rather than in observing them.
**Faster approach:** Use collocation recognition—test each option in the blank and choose the word that commonly pairs with "difficult to" in the context (e.g., "difficult to forecast").'),
  ('be687b35-06f4-52b7-9754-8cfdd3b17d88', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence says researchers have claimed to invalidate the phenomenon, arguing that it cannot be proven true because attempts to detect it involve faulty methods. "Substantiated" means supported with evidence, so the clause "cannot be substantiated" precisely expresses that the Hawthorne effect cannot be demonstrated or supported by reliable evidence—matching the reasoning about faulty research methods. The surrounding words ("invalidate," "attempts to detect," "faulty research methods") point to whether evidence can confirm the effect, so "substantiated" fits logically and semantically.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Rectified" means corrected or made right. The sentence concerns whether the phenomenon can be shown to exist, not whether it can be fixed, so "rectified" does not match the intended meaning.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Inculcated" means taught or instilled (usually ideas or values). The sentence is about evidence for the phenomenon, not about teaching it, so this choice is irrelevant.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Hypothesized" means proposed as a hypothesis. The Hawthorne effect was already proposed; the debate is about whether it can be proven given faulty methods. Saying it "cannot be hypothesized" would contradict the passage’s premise and is therefore inappropriate.
**Faster approach:** Identify the logical relationship signaled by surrounding words (here, "invalidate" and "attempts to detect ... faulty methods") and substitute each choice into the sentence to see which preserves that relationship; the correct verb will directly refer to being supported by evidence—"substantiated."'),
  ('0ca12792-8232-5227-9947-0ec0ed00855d', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage explains that modern birds have bone-connected air sacs similar to structures thought to have existed in certain extinct theropods and sauropods; saying paleontologists treat birds'' respiratory systems as "proxies for" those of Ornithomimus and Phuwiangosaurus correctly conveys that birds stand in as models or substitutes for the extinct animals'' systems.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Harbingers of" means signs that announce something to come, which doesn’t match the intended idea of using birds as representative models of extinct anatomy.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Emissaries for" suggests messengers sent on behalf of others, a figurative and inappropriate sense here.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Subordinates of" implies inferiority or hierarchy, which is not what the sentence intends.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence and choose the one that preserves the meaning "used as a model or stand-in"—that quickly yields "proxies for."'),
  ('f57f8493-2010-5ad3-8eca-9eb3c3f1c7b7', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage first states that innovation studies have overemphasized developments like glassmaking, producing a general interpretation that technological change implies increasing complexity; it then presents Erb-Satullo’s archaeological evidence (the decline in gold filigree despite flourishing gold metallurgy) as a counterexample showing that that interpretation is not always accurate.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not claim a near-consensus among researchers or emphasize controversy about a study; it describes a prevailing interpretation and then gives a counterexample to that interpretation.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not frame two contrasting claims by different researchers or present an explicit critique; it offers a counterexample to a broader interpretation rather than a direct critique of another researcher’s specific claim.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage does not summarize several studies about one invention and then add recent evidence; instead it challenges a general interpretation of technological progress by providing a counterexample from archaeological findings.
**Faster approach:** Identify the main claim in the first sentence (the general interpretation) and then check whether the rest of the passage supports, exemplifies, or contradicts that claim—if it contradicts, look for an answer describing a counterexample.'),
  ('5c506f66-a445-54c8-b55e-6e8aa206fe51', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage summarizes Melis et al.’s use of a CNN and then immediately notes that the model’s output "aligned with results of prior studies...—one of several indications... that the model accurately represents" the biomechanics. That wording presents the alignment as evidence supporting the model’s efficacy, which matches Choice A.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage mentions agreement with prior results but does not compare Melis et al.’s CNN results to results from other researchers’ models; it contrasts the CNN output with direct measurements, not other models.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not explain or justify why the authors relied on a CNN instead of direct measurement; it only reports that the CNN’s predictions agreed with prior direct measurements.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The text does not describe the process by which Melis et al. improved or "honed" their model’s accuracy; it only reports that the model’s output aligned with previous findings.
**Faster approach:** For questions about main purpose, locate the sentence that expresses the passage’s conclusion (here, that the model’s output aligning with prior results indicates accuracy) and choose the answer that restates that conclusion directly.'),
  ('efec263c-53b8-59a1-868a-7dcd9628ef05', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage states that Miguel "was hoping not only to meet us but to make the acquaintance of the poet himself at Mamá’s house," which directly says he wanted to be introduced both to the sisters and to their father, Nicolás Ureña.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not say Miguel had been taught everything by his brother or that he sought to learn from the sisters; it only says the brother told him who they were and that they were smart.
**Choice B is incorrect.** There is no mention that Miguel wanted to share his poems or request feedback; the text specifies his hope to meet the sisters and their father.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage does not indicate Miguel lacked formal instruction or intended to ask Nicolás Ureña to be his mentor; it only mentions his wish to make the poet’s acquaintance.
**Faster approach:** Locate the phrase that states the character’s motive (here, "hoping not only to meet us but to make the acquaintance of the poet himself") and match it to the answer that restates that motive directly.'),
  ('0b7fcec3-41f4-5e58-a81e-3d3236206fed', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage states that Hair pioneered “fast painting” and that many of his acolytes, “including Livingston Roberts, imitated the technique,” and that this imitation “accounts in part for the impressionistic qualities that are now synonymous with the group’s shared aesthetic.” That language most strongly supports the inference that Roberts’s works likely have the visual, impressionistic qualities regarded as typical of Florida Highwaymen paintings—qualities that Walker’s more detailed paintings lack.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage links Roberts to the group’s impressionistic qualities but does not claim his works are more aesthetically interesting than Walker’s.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage explicitly says Roberts imitated Hair’s technique; it does not support an inference that Roberts’s works are less derivative than typically acknowledged.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage describes a lack of precision only as a stylistic feature tied to fast painting and the group’s aesthetic, not as evidence that those works are inferior.
**Faster approach:** Use the explicit wording that X “imitated” Y’s technique and that this “accounts” for Z; infer directly that X’s works likely show Z. For these questions, map subject → technique → described effect and pick the choice that mirrors that chain.'),
  ('6ccbec76-fdbc-537b-b387-f2518de8ec02', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The bar chart shows the percentage of total penalties by reason for each year; the bars for “undistributed income” (failure to meet minimum charitable distributions) are the tallest in each year (about 80% in 2003, ~75% in 2004, and ~55% in 2005), which means foundations that filed Form 4720 collectively paid a larger share of penalties for undistributed income than for any other listed reason.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The choice compares penalties paid by organizations that filed Form 4720 to those that filed Form 990 and 4720, but the graph gives only percentages of penalties by reason among foundations that filed Form 4720; it does not provide any data about organizations that filed Form 990, so this comparison cannot be supported.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The choice addresses percentages of filers who filed Form 4720 for different reasons, but the graph reports percentages of total penalties assessed by reason, not percentages of filers; it does not show how many foundations filed for each reason.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The choice claims foundations paid larger average penalties for excess business holdings than for taxable expenditures, but the graph shows total-penalty percentages by reason (with “taxable expenditures” much larger than “excess business holdings”); it gives no information about average penalty per foundation, so this claim is unsupported.
**Faster approach:** Immediately read the y-axis label and interpret bars as “percentage of total penalties by reason.” Then scan which reason’s bars are highest across years — that reason is the one that collectively accounted for the largest penalties.'),
  ('21321c74-cc25-5635-b0e3-020b0b3c04d7', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The researchers argue that differences in pottery use reflect cultural differences rather than differences in local food availability; showing that people in both basins had broadly similar access to terrestrial and aquatic resources would remove environmental explanations and thus most directly support a cultural explanation for the different pottery uses.
**Choice A is incorrect.** If Volga people acquired cooking/storage pottery techniques from Don people, that would imply cultural transmission (not independent cultural differences) and would not support the claim that distinct local cultures independently produced different pottery uses.
**Choice C is incorrect.** More bodies of water in the Don basin would indicate different local resource availability and therefore would support an environmental, not cultural, explanation for the differing pottery uses.
**Choice D is incorrect.** If sites in both basins were seasonal fishing and hunting encampments, that would be consistent with similar subsistence patterns but would not by itself show that resource availability was the same; it does not directly rule out environmental explanations and so does not most directly support a cultural explanation.
**Faster approach:** Eliminate answer choices that point to environmental/resource differences; prefer the choice that shows resources were similar across regions, because that directly supports a cultural (rather than environmental) explanation.'),
  ('116559ba-b64e-55f3-932a-3cf6e48b9931', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The scientist’s conclusion depends on combining performance and practical considerations (notably cost). A says the BZMA-derived resin has only modestly lower absorption than the very expensive β-cyclodextrin resin but is better than other cheap methacrylate resins. That pattern yields the best practical trade-off: near-top performance while using a commercially available, less expensive monomer, directly supporting the claim that the BZMA resin has the greatest practical potential.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Superior performance for only one compound (toluene) does not show an overall practical advantage for both tested contaminants and so does not directly support the scientist’s general conclusion.
**Choice C is incorrect.** If the expensive β-cyclodextrin resin is best for toluene, that undermines the idea that the cheaper BZMA resin offers the best overall practical option.
**Choice D is incorrect.** D says BZMA is similar to other cheap resins and only slightly better than the expensive resin; that provides little reason to prefer BZMA on practical grounds over other inexpensive options and thus does not directly support the conclusion.
**Faster approach:** Eliminate choices that don’t show BZMA as the best practical trade-off (near-top performance plus lower cost); pick the option that pairs near-best absorption with commercially available/cheaper monomer.'),
  ('baa51b2f-52db-557a-9f1b-8d5fb5e4cd34', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage reports that seeds recruited many more people with the desired characteristics than would be expected by chance; the most logical explanation is that people tend to associate with others like themselves, so the seeds’ social networks would include a high proportion of people who share those characteristics. This directly accounts for the higher-than-chance recruitment of suitable participants.
**Choice A is incorrect.** If network members didn’t know the seeds’ desirable characteristics, that would make recruitment of matching people less likely, not more, and thus fails to explain the observed result.
**Choice B is incorrect.** If networks contained people who do not know one another and who share few characteristics, that would reduce, not increase, the proportion of suitable recruits.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The size of the seeds’ networks doesn’t explain why a larger-than-expected proportion of recruits had the desired characteristics; size alone could increase numbers but not the proportion matching the study criteria.
**Faster approach:** Look for the answer that restates the causal idea implied by the result (here, homophily —people’s networks contain others like themselves); choose the option that directly links similarity within networks to the higher-than-chance matches.'),
  ('17a5306f-04f4-57e7-a798-b5de65c12d5a', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The defenders say that reimaginings for new cultural contexts are nearly as old as the myths themselves; that point directly undercuts the purists’ claim that modern retellings uniquely impose contemporary values. Choice A restates that implication: elevating female perspectives is not evidence of a novel attitude about fidelity to ancient ideologies.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It contradicts the defenders’ point: they argue the practice is not recent, whereas B claims the complex motivations reflect a recent shift.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage speaks generally about reimagining myths, not specifically about a longstanding tradition of focusing on female characters; C overstates what the defenders’ evidence supports.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage offers no evidence about the modern writers’ chief motivations; D introduces an unsupported causal claim.
**Faster approach:** Identify the defenders’ purpose (deny novelty) and pick the choice that restates that denial; eliminate options that assert novelty, specifics not given, or unsupported motives.'),
  ('b4cc8132-908f-589c-b890-3055ce05829d', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The colon is the correct punctuation to introduce the examples that follow: the Hellisheidi, Darajat, and Pamukoren plants. The phrase becomes "...an array of technologies: in Iceland, the Hellisheidi plant uses...," where the colon signals that specific examples follow and the comma after "in Iceland" correctly sets off the introductory locative phrase.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "technologies, in Iceland," uses commas to set off a phrase but fails to signal that specific examples follow; it creates an awkward interruption and does not properly introduce the list of examples.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "technologies in Iceland:" places the colon after "Iceland," which implies that the examples are limited to Iceland; but the sentence lists plants in multiple countries, and more importantly it awkwardly attaches "in Iceland" to the noun phrase instead of using an introductory phrase for the first example.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "technologies in Iceland," creates a comma splice by failing to provide punctuation that introduces the following independent clause; it does not properly set up the examples that follow.
**Faster approach:** Use a colon to introduce specific examples that follow a general statement; insert "technologies:" whenever a list or examples immediately follow.'),
  ('5cc7a852-17b9-536e-86f9-e8ae2f71408b', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The antecedent of the blank is the singular noun phrase "a given term," so the pronoun must be singular. "It often becomes susceptible to obliteration by incorporation" correctly uses the singular pronoun "it" and the verb phrase "becomes susceptible," producing clear subject-verb agreement and natural wording.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "They often become" uses the plural pronoun "they," which does not agree with the singular antecedent "a given term."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "This often becomes" uses the demonstrative "this," which can sometimes refer to a whole idea but is less natural here than the direct singular pronoun "it" referring to "a given term." The expected, straightforward reference is "it."
**Choice D is incorrect.** "These often become" is plural and therefore does not agree with the singular antecedent "a given term."
**Faster approach:** Use pronoun-antecedent agreement: identify the antecedent’s number (singular or plural) and choose the matching singular or plural pronoun and verb form.'),
  ('5cfe54bf-7719-5862-a3b3-06b81cc620ab', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence needs no internal punctuation between "currency" and "in favor of a direct trade system" because the restrictive clause "that requires..." modifies "system." Reading the full sentence with choice A—"Barter economies eschew currency in favor of a direct trade system that requires..."—clearly conveys that the direct trade system (barter) requires the double coincidence of wants.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The em dash isolates "in favor of a direct trade system" as parenthetical and disrupts the straightforward noun phrase; it is unnecessary and less standard here.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The comma before "in favor" creates a nonrestrictive pause and makes the sentence sound awkward; it also risks misplacing the relative clause.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Adding a period after "system" would end the sentence prematurely and leave the clause "that requires..." dangling.
**Faster approach:** Check which noun the relative clause "that..." should modify; choose the punctuation that keeps that noun and the clause together (usually no punctuation for restrictive modifiers).'),
  ('74552689-020d-5fb2-a95a-0687797559e5', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence uses the noun phrase "A plano-convex lens" as the subject that performed the action described by the rest of the sentence; the present-participle phrase "focusing the laser on the center of the 10-millimeter-long crystal" correctly modifies that noun and describes the lens’s role, and the main verb "ensured" then links to the result (the spot size). The structure is equivalent to "A plano-convex lens, focusing the laser on the center ..., ensured a spot size ..." which is grammatical and clear.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Focused" is a past-tense verb; used here it would create a second finite verb without a conjunction ("A lens focused ... ensured"), producing a fused sentence and a grammatical error.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Focus" is the base form; it cannot function properly in this position without an additional auxiliary or relative word (e.g., "that focus" or "to focus").
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Focuses" is present-tense and would create subject–verb disagreement or require a different sentence structure ("A lens focuses ... ensured" is ungrammatical).
**Faster approach:** Recognize that a modifying phrase describing the lens should be nonfinite (a participial phrase); choose the present participle ("-ing") when you need to describe an ongoing role of the noun before the main verb.'),
  ('bed8d879-e34b-5616-9d9d-6b8e572ebe10', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence’s main subject is the singular noun "text," so it needs a finite verb that agrees in number: "text ... punctuates sixty-seven gelatin silver prints." This yields a complete main clause and allows the participial phrase "highlighting the collaborative, documentary nature of Frazier’s work" to modify that clause.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Punctuate" is the plural present form and does not agree with the singular subject "text."
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Punctuating" is a present participle; using it here would leave the sentence without a finite main verb and create a sentence fragment.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Are punctuated by" makes the plural "sixty-seven gelatin silver prints" the subject being acted upon, which reverses the intended meaning and also disagrees with the singular "text."
**Faster approach:** Identify the main subject and check for a required finite verb; eliminate participles that would make a fragment and eliminate verb forms that don’t agree in number with the subject.'),
  ('e0a4c753-cd86-58d0-9336-bab931a13723', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence requires a comma to set off the explanatory phrase beginning with "the former." "Species, the former in a repository at Washington, DC’s Museum of Natural History and the latter in a repository at the Netherlands’ Naturalis Biodiversity Center" correctly links the plural antecedent "their respective species" with the paired clarifying phrases "the former... and the latter..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** A semicolon before "the former" would be incorrect because what follows ("the former in a repository...") is not an independent clause and thus cannot properly follow a semicolon.
**Choice C is incorrect.** A period would create a sentence fragment: "The former in a repository..." lacks a main verb and cannot stand alone.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Adding "and" after the comma still leaves a second clause that is a fragment ("and the former in a repository...") rather than a complete clause with a verb.
**Faster approach:** When you see "the former... and the latter..." as clarifying phrases, punctuate them as nonrestrictive appositives with a comma (not a semicolon, period, or "and").'),
  ('00c04f0c-b73d-5fed-8881-a64a1653394b', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence lists three complex items (the sa with its appositive, the shesep with its appositive, and the khar with its appositive), so semicolons are used to separate those items: "the sa, a unit of area; the shesep, a unit of length; and the khar, a unit of volume." This punctuation makes the list clear and conforms to Standard English.
**Choice B is incorrect.** A colon after "area" would incorrectly introduce the next list item; the colon/semicolon combination here produces a punctuation mismatch and is not the standard way to separate complex list items.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Using only a comma before "the shesep" and a semicolon later creates an inconsistent separation of list items; complex items that contain commas should be separated by semicolons, not mixed punctuation.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Putting a semicolon between "the shesep" and "a unit of length" breaks the appositive phrase and incorrectly separates a noun from its modifier; that punctuation is ungrammatical in this context.
**Faster approach:** When a list’s items contain internal commas (appositives), choose semicolons to separate the main items—look for where commas already appear and use semicolons between those larger units.'),
  ('c8a16290-ca67-55d7-8075-ec57ddeeaefd', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence sets up a contrast between most animals migrating at night and poisonous amphibians migrating during the day; the transition must signal that the following clause describes what occurs specifically "at that time" (i.e., during daytime migration). "At that time" logically links the daytime context to the result that their color patterns deter predators, so the sentence reads smoothly: because these amphibians migrate while the sun is up, their bright colors function as a daytime warning.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "In other words" is used to restate or clarify a previous idea in simpler terms. The second clause does not restate the first clause; it gives a causal connection tied to the daytime context, so a restatement marker is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "However" signals contrast. The two clauses are not in opposition—the second clause explains why daytime migration is safe for these amphibians—so a contrasting transition misrepresents the relationship.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "For example" introduces an illustration of a general claim. The second clause is not an example of the first; it explains what happens during daytime migration, so an exemplifying transition is not suitable.
**Faster approach:** Identify the relationship between the two clauses (contrast, cause, example, restatement). Then pick the transition that names that relationship; here the second clause explains what happens during "that time," so choose a temporal/causal connector like "At that time."'),
  ('e1874d5b-6951-57dd-87f6-3994f5f1f5ea', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence that follows explains an expected fact about the missing interval: that time passed and sediments were removed by erosion. "Of course," signals that what follows is an obvious explanation for the gap, so it fits the tone and logic of the sentence: "Of course, time did not stand still during these intervening years; the unaccounted-for sedimentary material was likely removed..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "As a result," indicates a consequence of the preceding clause, but the sentence instead offers an explanation for the absence of rock, not a consequence of the adjacency of different-aged rocks.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "On the contrary," would assert a direct contradiction of the prior idea; the passage does not present an explicit claim that nothing happened during the gap to contradict, so this is too strong.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "In particular," would introduce a specific example related to the previous statement, but the following clause gives a general explanation rather than a particular instance, so it is not appropriate.
**Faster approach:** Identify whether the blank should signal expectation/obviousness, contrast, consequence, or exemplification; match the transition word to that function. For an explanation of a gap, choose a word signaling obviousness (e.g., "Of course").'),
  ('be59daeb-e7d6-5052-a152-b3ef9ff01781', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The first sentence establishes Gruen’s design idea (an indoor town square that encouraged strolling and lingering), and the second clause describes a specific design decision that follows from that idea: long promenades and seating areas. "Consequently," signals a logical result or consequence of Gruen’s conception, so it correctly links the general idea to the concrete features he implemented.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "For example," would treat the second clause as an illustration of the general claim, but the sentence sequence more strongly expresses causation (he conceived X; consequently he did Y), not merely illustration.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Regardless," indicates that the second clause occurs despite the first, which contradicts the intended causal relationship.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "In fact," offers emphasis or correction rather than showing a causal consequence; it does not clearly express that the design choices resulted from his conception.
**Faster approach:** Identify the logical relation between sentences (cause → effect, example, contrast, or emphasis); pick the transition that matches that relation—here, cause → effect so choose a consequence word.'),
  ('8737501a-ace5-5e19-b1c5-1a0993588a4c', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage presents an existing explanation (passive displacement by currents) for slight shifts in barnacles’ positions and then reports new findings that conflict with that explanation: the barnacles moved toward the turtles’ heads, i.e., against the prevailing water flow, which supports active movement. The phrase “Undermining this explanation,” explicitly signals that the new research calls the prior passive-displacement explanation into question and so fits logically and syntactically.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “Confirming this hypothesis,” asserts agreement with the previous explanation, but the new finding contradicts (rather than confirms) the passive-displacement account.
**Choice B is incorrect.** “Drawing a similar conclusion,” likewise indicates agreement; the new observation is opposite to the earlier conclusion and so cannot draw a similar one.
**Choice D is incorrect.** “Contrary to this phenomenon,” is less precise and slightly awkward: the contrast is with the earlier explanation for the phenomenon, not the phenomenon itself, making C the better-fit transition.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the following clause supports or opposes the previous claim; choose the transition that signals agreement or contradiction accordingly (eliminate the three that signal the wrong relation).'),
  ('0cb3f1f4-2929-552a-b0f5-0e5e0d35b52e', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence before the blank describes Dillard’s struggle to reconcile two opposing aspects of nature; the clause after the blank states the resulting understanding that the two aspects are linked. "Ultimately," signals a final, summarizing conclusion about Dillard’s view and therefore fits the logical flow.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "To that end" means "for that purpose," which would indicate intention or action taken to achieve something; the passage is presenting a conclusion, not an intended action, so this choice is inappropriate.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Hence" indicates a direct logical consequence, which is close, but it reads slightly more formal/causal than the summarizing tone of the passage; "Ultimately" more clearly conveys a final synthesis of Dillard’s thinking.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Moreover" adds an additional point, but the sentence that follows is not an extra detail so much as the culminating insight, so "Moreover" is not a good fit.
**Faster approach:** For transition questions asking for a concluding idea after a struggle or discussion, choose transitions that signal summary or final judgment (e.g., "Ultimately," "In sum," "Therefore") rather than those that signal purpose or mere addition.'),
  ('b3af866c-0eb6-5b4c-946e-6c7079becfc7', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence explicitly contrasts the status of the two films by noting that Thorpe’s 1929 film is "considered lost to history" while Micheaux’s 1920 film is "archived at the UCLA Film Television Archive in Los Angeles, California," directly using the provided facts to emphasize their difference in survival and availability.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it correctly states that Thorpe’s film is lost, it does not draw any comparison to Micheaux’s film and so fails to emphasize the difference between the two movies.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This choice treats both films as similar examples of 1920s silent films and therefore does not highlight the contrasting statuses (lost vs. archived) given in the sentences.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While true about Micheaux’s film being archived, it only gives one fact and does not emphasize the difference with Thorpe’s lost film.
**Faster approach:** For questions asking for emphasis on a difference, look for answer choices that use contrast words (e.g., "while," "whereas") and that state both facts to be compared; choose the option that explicitly contrasts the two items.'),
  ('385029ea-3ee9-5b94-b20a-722e2f443bee', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The notes state that EMOTIV develops BCIs, which "interpret and execute brain signals to allow users to control external software or hardware with their thoughts," and that Saluda Medical develops "neuromodulation technologies" that "use neural interfaces to stimulate nervous system structures and influence neural activity." Choice D accurately contrasts those two types by noting that BCIs enable thought-based control of external devices, whereas neuromodulation technologies act by influencing neural activity.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It misleadingly implies Saluda does not develop neurotechnology; the notes show both companies develop neurotechnology, so this choice fails to contrast the types.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It repeats the BCI definition awkwardly and incorrectly attributes restoration of sensory, motor, or neural functions to neuromodulation; those functions are described in the notes as the role of neuroprostheses, not neuromodulation.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It invents the term "neuroprosthodontic" and miscategorizes EMOTIV’s technology; the notes identify EMOTIV’s work as BCIs, not a newly named category, and also wrongly contrasts it with BrainGate.
**Faster approach:** Scan the note lines that define each term (BCIs vs. neuromodulation) and directly compare those short definitions—pick the answer that accurately restates the paired definitions without adding new or conflicting information.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

